INT. AIR RAID WARDEN POST - SCHOOLYARD SHELTER - NIGHT (1941)

A concrete box dug under a playground. A hurricane lamp, a field telephone, a rack of tin helmets, sandbags to the ceiling. Muffled drone overhead.

EDMUND HARTLEY, 50s, grocer by day, warden by night, white helmet, rests his hand on the phone. VERA PLUMMER, 20s, volunteer, clipboard and bicycle clips, sits on a crate with a mug of cocoa. ALF DOBSON, 60s, retired railwayman, hums a hymn to himself.

The drone grows. Edmund glances at the ceiling.

EDMUND
Mark it. Eleven forty.

VERA
Marked.

ALF
(muttering)
That's no Spitfire.

The field telephone rings. Edmund snatches it.

EDMUND
Post Seven.

CONTROL (V.O.)
Post Seven, this is Control. Report.

EDMUND
Nothing yet. Heard them over the canal.

CONTROL (V.O.)
Hold position. Fire watch at the gasworks says they're dropping flares.

EDMUND
Flares.

CONTROL (V.O.)
Prepare for incendiaries.

He hangs up. Looks at Vera and Alf.

EDMUND (CONT'D)
Right. Helmets on. Buckets by the door.

Vera sets down her cocoa. Alf rises, joints cracking.

VERA
Mr. Hartley, shouldn't we stay below?

EDMUND
Not for incendiaries.

ALF
You'll want sand, not water.

EDMUND
I know my business, Alf.

ALF
Nobody said you didn't.

A distant thud. Dust shivers from the ceiling.

The phone rings again. Edmund picks up.

CONTROL (V.O.)
Post Seven. Strike on the Albert Road. Third house from the corner.

Edmund goes pale.

EDMUND
That's my shop.

CONTROL (V.O.)
Say again?

EDMUND
That's my shop. That's my daughter's flat above it.

CONTROL (V.O.)
Warden Hartley, stay at your post.

He puts the phone down very carefully. Takes off his helmet. Puts it back on, tighter.

VERA
Mr. Hartley--

EDMUND
Vera. Take the register. Alf, you're with me.

ALF
Aye.

EDMUND
We're going up.

He opens the door. Night, red and roaring, floods in.
